assertion  XSH.modf.001
anchor     functions/modf.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/modf.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       modf(3.75), (-3.75), (5), (0.25), (-0.25), (100.5), (-2.5) and
           (0.0625) return the fractions 0.75, -0.75, 0, 0.25, -0.25, 0.5, -0.5
           and 0.0625 and store the integral parts 3, -3, 5, 0, 0, 100, -2 and 0.
test       t/001-values.c

assertion  XSH.modf.002
anchor     functions/modf.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/modf.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       modf(3) returns +0 and stores 3; modf(-3) returns -0 and stores -3;
           modf(0.25) stores +0; modf(-0.25) stores -0.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/002-zero-signs.c

assertion  XSH.modf.003
anchor     functions/modf.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/modf.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       modf(NaN, &ip) returns a NaN and stores a NaN in ip.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/003-nan.c

assertion  XSH.modf.004
anchor     functions/modf.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/modf.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       modf(+Inf, &ip) returns +0 and stores +Inf; modf(-Inf, &ip) returns -0
           and stores -Inf.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/004-infinity.c

assertion  XSH.modf.005
anchor     functions/modf.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/modf.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1988
says       With each rounding direction that can be selected (at least two are
           needed), modf(2.75) returns 0.75 and stores 2, and modf(-2.75) returns
           -0.75 and stores -2.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/005-rounding-mode.c

assertion  XSH.modf.006
anchor     functions/modf.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/modf.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.23
class      required
since      1988
says       With errno holding EDOM before the call, modf(3.75, &ip) leaves it
           unchanged and, when exception flags are in use, raises none of the
           invalid, divide-by-zero and overflow exceptions.
test       t/006-no-error.c
